From: CardioX signal-chain review, 30 August 2026
Subject: [PENDING CLINICAL SIGN-OFF] put RV5/SV1 on the same scale as the printed waveform

WHAT THIS CHANGES
  The measured RV5 and SV1 values printed on the report, and therefore the
  Sokolow-Lyon index compared against the 35 mm LVH threshold.

WHY IT IS NOT APPLIED
  It changes a reported clinical number. Commit 95abd7e already identified this
  and deferred it for the same reason. It needs Dr. Rahman's sign-off, and it
  should ship together with the bench calibration below, not before it.

THE DEFECT
  The waveform is drawn at ADC_PER_MV = 1184.0
    (src/ecg/ecg_report_android.py:59, src/ecg/twelve_lead_test.py:783)
  but the measurements use different, per-lead divisors:
    (src/ecg/clinical_measurements.py:601)
  RV5 is divided by 2048.0  ->  prints 57.8% of the drawn height
  SV1 is divided by 1441.0  ->  prints 82.2% of the drawn height

  No caller overrides these; src/ecg/metrics/intervals.py:213 calls the function
  without passing them, so 2048/1441 are what run. They date from 46dcd2f, the
  initial commit, and were never measured on this hardware -- the code comments
  describe them as "Adjusted calibration factor for GE/Philips alignment", i.e.
  hand-tuning to match another cart, per lead.

  Consequence: a clinician measuring Sokolow-Lyon with calipers off the printed
  trace gets a different answer from the number printed beside it. Verified on
  6 reports: the printed index is 1.45-1.56x smaller than the trace shows.

THE CHANGE
--- a/src/ecg/clinical_measurements.py
+++ b/src/ecg/clinical_measurements.py
@@ -600,7 +600,7 @@
 def measure_rv5_sv1_from_median_beat(v5_raw, v1_raw, r_peaks_v5, r_peaks_v1, fs,
-                                      v5_adc_per_mv=2048.0, v1_adc_per_mv=1441.0):
+                                      v5_adc_per_mv=1184.0, v1_adc_per_mv=1184.0):

  Also review the duplicate legacy block using the same divisors at
  src/ecg/twelve_lead_test.py:4016 and :4065 -- determine whether it is live,
  then align or delete it.

EXPECTED EFFECT
  Printed RV5 rises by 2048/1184 = 1.73x, SV1 by 1441/1184 = 1.22x.
  Worked example, report A989_20260829_110042 ("qq"):
    printed today          RV5 1.999   SV1 0.000   index 1.999 mV (20.0 mm)
    after this change      RV5 1.851   SV1 1.185   index 3.036 mV (30.4 mm)
  against a 35 mm threshold. (SV1 also stops being 0.000 -- that part is already
  fixed separately; the value above is measured from the raw V1 of that record,
  present on 11 of 11 beats.)

MUST SHIP WITH
  A bench calibration. 1184 itself is not settled: commit 2306635 records lead II
  matching a reference cart at 10 small boxes at 1184 ADC/mV, while V2 reads 14.4
  boxes against 11 on the same cart -- about 31% apart. One scalar cannot satisfy
  both anchors.

  The device has EIGHT real front-end channels (I, II, V1-V6); III/aVR/aVL/aVF are
  derived in software (verified: III = II - I is bit-exact). So lead II and V2 are
  two physically separate amplifiers, and a per-channel gain difference is a real
  possibility rather than a software artifact.

  TEST: inject a known 1 mV into a limb channel and into a chest channel
  separately and compare ADC counts.
    equal      -> 1184 stands for all channels; apply this patch as written.
    different  -> per-channel constants are needed; this patch becomes the
                  starting point, not the answer.
